From 58ffeb00ec73ffd6d4b73663702672a853e74b22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:01:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] 3D: each eye's video copied small by the
 graphics and measured on a thread of its own

The depth of a 3D video has to be measured from what is drawn, whatever
decoded it (the processor, VA-API, NVDEC) and whatever the layout of the
file, without slowing the playback down.

CStereoDepthCapture copies, right after each eye's video was drawn into
the framebuffer and before anything is drawn over it, the video's
rectangle of that eye into a picture of 480 x 270, halving it in steps
of two with framebuffer blits (each step averages 2 x 2 pixels, so a
large picture is reduced without aliasing), the two eyes one above the
other. Once both are in, the graphics read them into a pixel buffer
behind a fence; frames later the render thread takes a read that is
done and copies it (1 MB). It never waits for the graphics. It needs
OpenGL (ES) 3; a framebuffer of floating point, a failed blit or read,
end it for the video, and say why.

CStereoDepthMeter, which the render manager will own: AfterVideo() for
each eye's pass (only where the eye's viewport is the one
SetStereoView() set, at most every 75 ms and once per picture);
Frame() once a frame: the reads go to a thread of its own that makes
the light of them and matches them (StereoDepth.cpp), and the planner
gives the depths of the frame. It measures only where it can: an
automatic setting, a split 3D output (side by side, top and bottom,
frame packing), a video drawn in each eye's pass (not an HDR one,
drawn once under the interface), full screen. kodi.log says once where
the depth comes from: the disc's offsets, measured, or the setting, and
why it is not measured.

On Mesa's software OpenGL ES (llvmpipe), where the processor does the
work of the graphics, the render thread spent 4 to 8 ms a frame; on
graphics it is a few blits and a 1 MB copy (about 250 MB/s of reads of
the graphics' memory at 1080p per eye).
